# Worktree hygiene — the tested tree is the shipped tree

Concurrent lanes each work in their own `git worktree` (`06-branching.mdc`).
A tracked file rewritten on disk while an editor buffer holds it — by
`cargo fmt`, or by a script that reads the file and writes it back whole —
reverts to the buffer's older bytes at a later flush. Nothing announces
the flush. The interval is arbitrary. `git status` after the rewrite is
what catches it.

The dangerous case is a drift followed by `git add -A` or `git commit -a`.
The stale bytes are committed, and the tree that was tested is not the
tree that ships. What gets committed is the text the verification existed
to remove.

## Three constraints

1. **Stage explicit paths.** Never `git add -A`, `git add .`, `git add -u`,
   or `git commit -a` in a worktree left open between edits. Every commit
   names the files it carries. If a file you did not edit shows as
   modified, read the diff. Restore it from `HEAD` (`git checkout -- <path>`)
   when it is a drift. Before staging a file you did edit, diff it against
   `HEAD` and read every hunk you did not make. Explicit paths are not
   enough: a file that is legitimately in the commit can still be carrying
   a reverted hunk.

2. **Verify from a fresh checkout of the pushed SHA, not from the worktree.**
   Before reporting a PR head as green, and before resolving review
   findings against it, run the gates in a checkout that contains only
   what was pushed. The verified SHA is the tip you are asking to merge.
   Re-run after any merge that resolved a conflict: a conflict resolution
   is code neither parent contains, so neither parent's green covers it.
   State the SHA the run covered.

   ```bash
   git clone -q --no-checkout --shared <repo> /tmp/verify-<pr>
   cd /tmp/verify-<pr> && git fetch -q origin <branch> && git checkout -q <sha>
   git submodule update --init --recursive -q
   env -u CARGO_TARGET_DIR cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings
   env -u CARGO_TARGET_DIR cargo test ...
   ```

   The clone's build must be the clone's. A shared `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`
   compiles the clone into the worktree's artifacts. `env -u CARGO_TARGET_DIR`
   on every cargo invocation in the clone is the isolation. Check
   `echo ${CARGO_TARGET_DIR:-unset}` before trusting a clone result.

   A number quoted from a run comes from that run's own log. Two runs
   writing one log, or a `grep` over an interleaved file, produce a figure
   that is green and wrong. One run, one log. When a gate's figure is
   quoted, name the gate and the commit that landed it the first time.

3. **Read back anything you publish.** A PR body, a review comment, an
   issue, a gist — any write whose destination is outside the repository
   is verified by reading the destination back and finding the lines the
   edit added, never by the sending command's exit status. An untracked
   body file drifts like a tracked one and has no `HEAD` to differ from;
   constraints 1 and 2 cannot see this class — there is no diff to read
   and no SHA to clone — so the check has to be at the destination
   (`gh pr view --json body` after `gh pr edit`, and `grep` the read-back
   for the lines the edit added).

## The edit form that notices

An anchored edit — `StrReplace` with enough context to be unique, a script
that asserts its old text occurs once — fails when the file is not the
text it was written against, and writes nothing. A line-number edit or a
wholesale write succeeds against whatever is there, including a drifted
file, and succeeds silently.

- Anchor every edit to the text it expects. Prefer the edit tool over
  line-addressed or whole-file writes for a file that has been sitting in
  a worktree. When a wholesale write is unavoidable, diff the result
  against `HEAD` and read every hunk you did not intend.
- A script that reads a tracked file and writes it back whole is an
  out-of-editor rewrite even when the replacement is anchored. Check
  `git status` after it, as after `cargo fmt`.
- Untracked files drift the same way and have no `HEAD` to restore from.
  The reverted text is simply what is there.
- An anchored edit that fails to match is a finding. Do not widen the
  anchor or retarget against whatever is there now. Run `git diff` first.
  If the current text differs from `HEAD` in lines you did not touch this
  session, restore the file, then edit.

A green gate is evidence the tree is well-formed. It is not evidence the
tree is the one you edited. A drifted-but-valid file still compiles.

CI runs on the pushed SHA, so a drift that breaks a gate fails there.
This rule covers the drift that passes gates while being wrong, and the
window between "I ran the tests" and "CI is green."

